{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, Q2, the cap: the queue holds AT MOST ONE pending invocation, and further intervals arriving while it is full COALESCE into it rather than accumulating (spec/interaction-events.md:188-195). Pinning a cap needs a handler that is blocked while several of its intervals arrive and is then FREE, with no fresh interval, across several checkpoints -- which a self-blocking `every` body cannot provide, because each drained invocation immediately rebuilds the backlog. The blocker here is therefore an `on_key` handler, which fires once for the one key the host delivers. Measured: the key arrives at tick 10, on the same tick the `every 10` handler is due, so the tick's whole batch is claimed up front (spec/interaction-events.md:84-89 orders `on_key` before due `every`) and the `every` handler sits CLAIMED while the `on_key` body's `wait 25` runs ticks 11-35. Two of its intervals arrive in that window, at ticks 20 and 30, and coalesce into the one slot. The `on_key` body ends at tick 35; the `every` handler then runs from its original claim (print 1). The first `wait 0` yields at tick 35 -- no clock advance, and the handler is not due until tick 40 -- so the ONLY thing that can fire is the queued occurrence (print 2). The second and third `wait 0` yield at that same tick and fire NOTHING, which is the cap itself: two arrivals, one drain, empty. Measured against a runtime whose queue is an uncapped counter, the same program prints THREE times -- the unbounded buildup the sentence exists to forbid. It also fails a fixed-DELAY scheduler, which prints once.",
